#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074893} :activity/id #uuid "48334486-8ae7-5572-8e8f-b176d6318f35" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050138 :topic/id #uuid "2fda426e-c98b-5b36-800a-304f9ce90e0d" :topic/math-academy-id 554} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074893 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075038 :tutorial/content "Let's take a look at the following equivalent ratios:\n\n$$\n00\n$$\n\nWe can place the equivalent ratios in a **ratio table**, such as the one below. In a ratio table, each row represents a ratio.\n\n| ${\\color{blue}5}$ | ${\\color{red}10}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| ${\\color{blue}10}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}\\textrm{?}}$ |\n| ${\\color{blue}20}$ | ${\\color{red}40}$ |\n\nOne number is missing. Let's figure out what it is.\n\nWe start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}5}:{\\color{red}10}$, which is the first ratio in the table. Writing this ratio as a fraction, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}5}}{{\\color{red}10}}\n$$\n\nThe numerator represents the left-hand entry (${\\color{blue}5}$), and the denominator represents the right-hand entry (${\\color{red}10}$).\n\nTo find the missing number, we need to make a numerator of ${\\color{blue}10}$. We can do this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5 \\cdot 2}{10 \\cdot 2} &= \\frac{10}{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now add this to our ratio table:\n\n| ${\\color{blue}5}$ | ${\\color{red}10}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| ${\\color{blue}10}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}20}$ |\n| ${\\color{blue}20}$ | ${\\color{red}40}$ |\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\color{red}20$." :tutorial/id #uuid "ca7b3d51-7281-5705-bed5-276f34939c25" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8357 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ac8583b6-39eb-5a51-9b51-c3c4ebec13fa" :step/math-academy-id 81656 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074894}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074894 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073894 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073453 :question/id #uuid "8e2f5bec-3573-5f1d-9870-41ac26b1ddf2" :question/math-academy-id "e-13342"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9357cc9-e383-5d88-9741-f49f6fbcca99" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050481}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074049}] :knowledge-point/title "Filling a Ratio Table by Multiplying: Missing Denominators"} :step/id #uuid "f054590d-449e-5753-b1fc-569c01699cad" :step/math-academy-id 81657 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074895}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074895 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073895 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073455 :question/id #uuid "e2ada4db-c1f0-53b0-9af7-ee2712ae75ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-13409"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e3287b0-2ecb-5d83-9596-7943d68f8884" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074384}] :knowledge-point/title "Filling a Ratio Table by Multiplying: Missing Numerators"} :step/id #uuid "aab5d8f4-9d49-5c7f-9619-01557ca3969b" :step/math-academy-id 81660 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074896}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074896 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073896 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073454 :question/id #uuid "11fac5c9-ca4a-54a6-9f38-3c675411673e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13343"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60021495-9d2a-58a5-9321-bb16013bbba7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074050}] :knowledge-point/title "Filling a Ratio Table by Multiplying and Dividing"} :step/id #uuid "bd74231b-139c-5091-8fd3-66103c7f1329" :step/math-academy-id 81663 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074897}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074897 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073897 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073456 :question/id #uuid "f1a6757